Q: pset2 vigenere help (Segmentation Fault) A: <p>There are multiple problems in your code:</p>
<ul>
<li>[Minor] <code>string k = argv[1];</code> should be moved after the test for <code>argc == 2</code>: you should first check for the proper number of arguments.</li>
<li>[Major] <code>isalpha(k)</code> is a real problem: you pass the <code>string</code> argument <code>k</code>, which is a pointer to <code>char</code> where <code>isalpha</code> expects an <code>int</code> with a value between <code>0</code> and <code>255</code> or the special value <code>EOF</code>.  <code>isalpha</code> has undefined behavior for other values.  Passing a pointer is incorrect and triggers undefined behavior, which may result in a segmentation fault (ie: accessing memory unavailable to the current process).</li>
<li>[Minor] you should check that the argument string contains only letters and at least one.</li>
<li>[Major] <code>get_string</code> takes the prompt string as an argument</li>
<li>[Minor] <code>strlen(k)</code> should be computed just once instead of multiple times for each character in the input.</li>
<li>[Major] <code>j++</code> the index into the key should only be updated for actual letters, not for other characters, which should be output unchanged.</li>
<li>[Minor] <code>i</code>, <code>j</code> and <code>n</code> should be defined with type <code>size_t</code> to accommodate for huge input strings.</li>
<li>[Major] <code>p[i] + (k[j % strlen(k)] - 65)</code> is not the correct formula to shift the character in <code>p[i]</code> according to the rank of the character in <code>k[j % strlen(k)]</code>: you should first subtract the value of <code>'A'</code> from <code>p[i]</code> then add the offset computed as <code>(k[j % strlen(k)] - 'A')</code>, then reduce modulo 26, then add <code>'A'</code> to get an uppercase letter.  The other formulas should be modified according to the case of <code>p[i]</code> and <code>k[j % strlen(k)]</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here is a modified version addressing the issues here-above:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cs50.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

int main(int argc, string argv[])
{
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf(&quot;program requires a single argument\n&quot;);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }
    string k = argv[1];
    size_t klen = strlen(k);
    if (klen == 0) {
        printf(&quot;invalid key: must not be empty\n&quot;);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }
    for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; klen; i++) {
        unsigned char c = k[i];
        if (!isalpha(c)) {
            printf(&quot;invalid key: must contain only letters\n&quot;);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
    }
    string p = get_string(&quot;plaintext: &quot;);
    size_t plen = strlen(p);
    printf(&quot;ciphertext: &quot;);

    for (size_t i = 0, j = 0; i &lt; plen; i++) {
        unsigned c = p[i];
        if (isalpha(c)) {
            unsigned char ck = k[j];
            j = (j + 1) % klen;
            if (isupper(c)) {
                putchar('A' + (c - 'A' + toupper(ck) - 'A') % 26);
            } else {
                putchar('a' + (c - 'a' + toupper(ck) - 'A') % 26);
            }
        } else {
            putchar(c);
        }
    }
    printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
</code></pre>

Q: This &#39;onError&#39; handler must return a value assignable to &#39;UserCredential&#39;, but ends without returning a value. Try adding a return statement A: <p><code>FirebaseAuth.instance.signInWithEmailAndPassword(...)</code> returns a:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>Future&lt;UserCredential&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>When you call:</p>
<pre><code>.catchError((e) {
  associateMethods.showSnackBarMsg(&quot;Error occured: \n $e&quot;, context);
})
</code></pre>
<p>the <code>catchError</code> callback must return a <code>UserCredential</code>, because it is replacing the failed future.<br />
However, your handler returns nothing (<code>void</code>), so Dart throws the mentioned error of yours.
I think you could just remove catcheError and let the try/catch handle the error.</p>
